{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-sonnet-5-5", "id": "msg_011CfcU3D6jL41b621r78yzs", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "**Lists** are mutable. **Tuples** are immutable.\n\n| | List | Tuple |\n|---|---|---|\n| Syntax | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` (a single-item tuple needs a trailing comma: `(1,)`) |\n| Mutability | You can add, remove or change items | Fixed after creation |\n| Methods | `append`, `extend`, `pop`, `sort`, etc. | Only `count` and `index` |\n| Hashable | No, so it can't be a dict key or set member | Yes, if all its items are hashable |\n| Memory and speed | Slightly more overhead | Slightly smaller and faster to create |\n\n**When to use each**\n- **List:** a collection that grows, shrinks or gets reordered, usually of items of the same kind, such as a list of transactions.\n- **Tuple:** a fixed record whose positions have meaning, such as `(date, amount, currency)`. Tuples are also what you use for dict keys, for multiple return values, and for data you don't want changed by accident.\n\n```python\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nnums[0] = 99          # fine\n\npoint = (1, 2)\npoint[0] = 99         # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n```\n\nA tuple is only shallowly immutable. If it holds a mutable object, that object can still change:\n\n```python\nt = ([1, 2], 3)\nt[0].append(4)        # works: t is now ([1, 2, 4], 3)\n```"}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 7612, "cache_read_input_tokens": 16975, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 7612}, "output_tokens": 6, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "not_available"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "78c82d1a-773d-46dc-8c90-c51e41c0e173", "uuid": "0c1faddd-123c-465e-9935-472c8630293a",