end`, `pop`, `sort`, etc. | Only `count` and `index` |\n| Hashable | No, so it can't be a dict key or set member | Yes, if all its items are hashable |\n| Memory and speed | Slightly more overhead | Slightly smaller and faster to create |\n\n**When to use each**\n- **List:** a collection that grows, shrinks or gets reordered, usually of items of the same kind, such as a list of transactions.\n- **Tuple:** a fixed record whose positions have meaning, such as `(date, amount, currency)`. Tuples are also what you use for dict keys, for multiple return values, and for data you don't want changed by accident.\n\n```python\nnums = [1, 2, 3]\nnums[0] = 99          # fine\n\npoint = (1, 2)\npoint[0] = 99         # TypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\n```\n\nA tuple is only shallowly immutable. If it holds a mutable object, that object can still change:\n\n```python\nt = ([1, 2], 3)\nt[0].append(4)        # works: t is now ([1, 2, 4], 3)\n```", "ttft_ms": 2074, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 5583, "uuid": "d63e3e6b-22fd-4f13-aeee-6db5812141d8", "ttft_stream_ms": 1944, "time_to_request_ms": 421, "first_content_frame_ms": 1944, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 0}
